<ExtractVariables name="EV-ModelFromPath" continueOnError="false" enabled="true">
  <DisplayName>Extract the model id from the request path</DisplayName>
  <!--
    LLMTokenQuota needs a model name and there is NOWHERE in the request body
    to get one. A Vertex `generateContent` body is {contents, generationConfig,
    tools} only; the model lives in the URL:

      /v1/publishers/google/models/gemini-3.5-flash:generateContent

    Both LTQ policies originally used {jsonPath('$.model', request.content)},
    copied from docs/21. That resolves to nothing, and LLMTokenQuota treats an
    unresolvable model as a hard error rather than skipping:

      500 policies.llmtokenquota.FailedToResolveModelName
      "Failed to resolve model name {jsonPath('$.model', request.content, true)}
       for LLM Token Quota processing"

    which failed every single call. Confirmed live 2026-09-15 on the first real
    traffic through this gateway, see docs/BUILD-LOG.md #39. The response body
    is no better: Vertex returns `modelVersion`, not `model`.

    This runs in the request flow and the extracted variable stays in scope for
    the response flow, so LTQ-Count uses the same value, so request and response
    cannot then disagree about which model was billed.

    Note this is a legitimate use of ExtractVariables, unlike the EV-UserClaims
    bug in docs/BUILD-LOG.md #37: it has a real <Pattern> and extracts from
    message content. Copying an already-decoded value still belongs in an
    AssignMessage.
  -->
  <Source>request</Source>
  <URIPath>
    <!-- ** spans the BasePath and any publisher prefix, so this keeps working
         for publishers/google/... and any future publisher path shape. -->
    <Pattern ignoreCase="true">/**/models/{model}:{method}</Pattern>
  </URIPath>
  <VariablePrefix>llm</VariablePrefix>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</ExtractVariables>
